 # Surface and Geometry Alignment
+
+Also called: Geometric Priors in Gaussian Splatting, Surface-Aligned Gaussian Optimization
+
 ## Overview
-This family of methods addresses the fundamental limitation of vanilla 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS): the production of "floating" volumetric ellipsoids that lack an explicit, watertight geometric boundary [G]. By aligning the discrete Gaussian representation with continuous geometric priors (such as SDFs, depth maps, or explicit meshes), these methods enforce positional and normal consistency to enable accurate mesh extraction, physically-based rendering, and robust initialization [G]. Unlike sibling methods that may rely solely on photometric loss or implicit constraints, this family explicitly integrates geometric supervision into the training or post-processing pipeline to ensure the Gaussians conform to physical surfaces [G].
+This family of methods addresses the lack of explicit, watertight geometry in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), which consists of floating, semi-transparent ellipsoids . By aligning the discrete Gaussian representation with continuous geometric priors—such as signed distance fields (SDFs), metric depth maps, or explicit meshes—these techniques enforce positional and normal consistency to produce high-fidelity surface representations . Unlike sibling methods that primarily focus on training-time regularization or post-hoc mesh extraction, this family encompasses a spectrum from re-initialization strategies and physically-based inverse rendering to contrastive token alignment, enabling applications ranging from editable mesh generation to accurate material decomposition .
+
 ## Shared principles
-See [Geometry and Surface Priors](9270-geometry-and-surface-priors.md) for the shared mechanism, terms, and math [G].
+All methods in this family align the discrete Gaussian representation with a continuous geometric prior . The core mechanism adds geometric loss terms to the standard photometric rendering loss $\mathcal{L}_{rgb}$ . These losses enforce two properties: positional consistency, where Gaussian centers $\mathbf{c}_i$ are encouraged to lie on the zero-level set of a distance function (SDF or UDF) or align with metric depth estimates , and normal consistency, where the orientation of the Gaussians (derived from their covariance matrix or rotation quaternion) is aligned with the surface normal estimated from the geometric prior . The total loss function generally takes the form:
+$$ \mathcal{L}_{total} = \mathcal{L}_{rgb} + \lambda_{geo} \mathcal{L}_{geo} + \lambda_{norm} \mathcal{L}_{norm} $$
+where $\mathcal{L}_{geo}$ is the geometric consistency loss and $\mathcal{L}_{norm}$ is the normal consistency loss . The weights $\lambda_{geo}$ and $\lambda_{norm}$ are hyperparameters that balance photometric fidelity against geometric accuracy [G].
+
 ## Subtopics
-[SuGaR](9191-sugar.md) is a post-hoc surface alignment method that converts the discrete 3DGS representation into a continuous, watertight triangle mesh via self-regularization and Poisson reconstruction. It differs from siblings by operating after initial training rather than during, solving the "floating" ellipsoid problem for downstream mesh applications like animation or physics. Choose it when explicit mesh extraction is required without modifying the training pipeline.
 
-[Surface-guided Gaussian Re-initialization](9199-surface-guided-gaussian-re-initialization.md) is a training optimization that re-projects Gaussians onto a canonical surface mesh to resolve unbalanced aggregation caused by densification and splitting operations. It differs from siblings by explicitly repositioning Gaussians to a known geometry rather than relying on loss-driven alignment. Choose it when Gaussian distribution is uneven or clustered excessively during training.
+### [SuGaR](9191-sugar.md)
+A post-hoc method that converts the discrete, volumetric 3DGS representation into a continuous, editable triangle mesh by aligning Gaussians with a reconstructed surface. It solves the problem that vanilla 3DGS produces floating ellipsoids with no explicit geometry, making it difficult to use in standard DCC pipelines for animation or physics. Unlike sibling methods that modify the training loop or use implicit fields, SuGaR operates after training, using a self-regularization loss to flatten Gaussians onto a surface and then extracting a mesh via Poisson reconstruction.
 
-[Geo-Splatting](9201-geo-splatting.md) is a physically-based inverse rendering method that differentiably constructs surface-aligned Gaussians from an explicit mesh to improve normal estimation and light transport modeling. It differs from siblings by using BVH-accelerated ray tracing on the mesh for accurate PBR, avoiding the approximated normals and semi-transparent nature of standard Gaussian points. Choose it when accurate material decomposition, relighting, or self-occlusion modeling is required.
+### [Surface-guided Gaussian Re-initialization](9199-surface-guided-gaussian-re-initialization.md)
+A training strategy for 3DGS that resolves the unbalanced aggregation of Gaussians caused by densification and splitting operations. It makes trained Gaussians better fit the input monocular video by anchoring them to a surface representation. It is distinct from [SuGaR](9191-sugar.md) and [Geo-Splatting](9201-geo-splatting.md) by specifically targeting the re-initialization phase to correct aggregation artifacts rather than just loss regularization or initial placement.
 
-[GeoCE Loss](17334-geoce-loss.md) is a geometric contrastive embedding method that aligns token similarity with 3D proximity to ensure clustering by physical surface rather than viewpoint. It differs from siblings by using soft contrastive targets derived from unprojected depths to shape the latent space, rather than enforcing hard positional or normal constraints. Choose it when feature-space similarity needs to be regularized by explicit 3D geometric consistency.
+### [Geo-Splatting](9201-geo-splatting.md)
+A physically-based inverse rendering method that augments 3DGS with explicit geometric priors to improve normal estimation and light transport modeling. It solves the problem of inaccurate material decomposition and flawed relighting caused by approximated normals and semi-transparent Gaussian points in existing methods. Unlike siblings that rely on implicit constraints or low-frequency spherical Gaussians, Geo-Splatting differentiably constructs surface-aligned Gaussian points from an optimizable explicit mesh, leveraging well-defined mesh normals and opaque surfaces for superior decomposition and relighting quality.
+
+### [GeoCE Loss](17334-geoce-loss.md)
+A geometric regularization term used in ZipSplat to align token similarity with 3D proximity, ensuring that clustering merges image patches observing the same surface rather than grouping them by viewpoint. It solves the problem where feature-space merging does not guarantee geometric consistency, explicitly shaping the token space so that clusters correspond to physical surfaces. Unlike [Cone Loss](17337-cone-loss.md), which anchors individual tokens to their source patches, GeoCE handles token merging by enforcing that similar tokens are also 3D-proximate.
+
 ## Background
-The concept of aligning Gaussian representations with geometric priors emerged as a solution to the lack of explicit geometry in 3D Gaussian Splatting [G]. While 3DGS excels at novel view synthesis, its volumetric nature makes mesh extraction difficult [G]. Early works like [SuGaR](9191-sugar.md) introduced post-hoc regularization to flatten Gaussians into surfels [G]. Subsequent methods like [Geo-Splatting](9201-geo-splatting.md) integrated explicit meshes for physically-based rendering, while others like [GeoCE Loss](17334-geoce-loss.md) focused on feature-space geometric consistency [G]. The family continues to evolve with methods addressing initialization and sparse-view constraints [G].
-## Sources
-Not given in the sources.+The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting method  provides fast, high-quality novel view synthesis but lacks an explicit surface representation, consisting instead of a collection of anisotropic Gaussians. This limitation hinders applications requiring watertight meshes, such as physics simulation, texturing, and animation. Early attempts to extract geometry from 3DGS relied on post-processing techniques like Poisson reconstruction on the Gaussian centers, often resulting in noisy or incomplete meshes due to the volumetric nature of the representation . To address this, subsequent research introduced geometric priors during the optimization process. Methods like NeuSG and GaussianPro integrated implicit neural fields or depth priors to guide the Gaussian placement . More recent approaches, such as Geo-Splatting, have moved towards explicit mesh-based proxies for physically-based rendering, while others like SuGaR have refined post-hoc alignment to produce clean, editable meshes . The introduction of contrastive losses like GeoCE further refined the geometric consistency of token-based Gaussian representations, ensuring that learned features correspond to physical surfaces rather than viewpoint-dependent artifacts .
